  message: "DONE for socket/bind/listen (PR #602); `accept` RULED AND KEPT.\n\nREMOVED, 10 languages: c/cpp sys/socket {socket,bind,listen}; haskell\nNetwork.Socket {socket,bind,listen}; erlang/elixir gen_tcp/gen_sctp/ssl listen\nAND gen_udp.open; java/kotlin/scala ServerSocket.bind + DatagramSocket.bind;\nrust TcpListener::bind + UdpSocket::bind.\n\nEACH REMOVAL PASSED THE SAME TEST: a genuine transfer call is still rowed for\nthat channel, verified per language before any row was touched. Pinned by\ntest_every_f2_language_keeps_a_real_receive.\n\n`accept` KEPT and ruled, not overlooked: it returns the peer address, which is\nchosen by whoever connected, so it is real network-controlled data.\n\nJAVASCRIPT EXCLUDED, and this is the interesting part: its ENTIRE net_recv\nsurface is callback registration (http.createServer, Deno.listen/listenTls/\nlistenDatagram, WebSocket.onmessage, EventSource.addEventListener). Not one row\nis a transfer call, because in Node and the browser there IS no call site at\nwhich bytes arrive. Removing Deno.listen would be arbitrary beside listenTls and\nwould relocate the read to nothing. Same rule that keeps Django in WI-hikut.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE: git UNTRUSTED-NO-HOST-FS violated(5) -> inconclusive(0),\nall 5 sourced from `socket`. ejabberd violated(5) -> violated(3): the 2 removed\nare gen_udp.open and gen_tcp.listen, the 3 kept are genuine ETS reads -- the fix\nnarrows on evidence rather than blanket-clearing.\n\nALSO NOTED, NOT FIXED (file separately if it matters): javascript declares\nBroadcastChannel.postMessage under net_recv, and WebSocket.onopen/onclose/\nonerror are lifecycle events rather than data receives."  # 99b7
